+  <article class="journal" id="roasters-journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+    <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+    <p class="byline">Notes from the roastery floor &mdash; how our beans find their way to your cup</p>
+    <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1447933601403-0c6688de566e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=80"
+      alt="Freshly roasted coffee beans spilling across a wooden surface" width="1400" height="613" loading="lazy">
+
+    <div class="summary-panel">
+      <button type="button" class="btn" id="summarize-btn" disabled>Summarize this story</button>
+      <p class="summary-status" id="summary-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
+      <ul class="summary-output" id="summary-output" aria-label="Summary of the Roaster's Journal"></ul>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="journal-body" id="journal-body">
+      <p>Every bag of coffee we roast begins long before it reaches our drum roaster on Mill Street. It starts on a hillside, often thousands of feet above sea level, with a farmer who has spent years&mdash;sometimes generations&mdash;learning exactly when a coffee cherry is ready to be picked. Our job, as we see it, is to honor that work: to find those farmers, build lasting relationships with them, and then do as little as possible to get in the way of what they've grown.</p>
+
+      <h3>Finding the farms</h3>
+      <p>We don't buy coffee off a commodity exchange. Instead, our green coffee buyer spends roughly three months of every year traveling to origin, visiting farms and washing stations in Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, Peru, and Sumatra. Many of the producers we work with today were introduced to us by other farmers, by local cooperatives, or by importers who share our commitment to traceability. Before we commit to a new partner, we want to walk their land, see their processing setup, and understand the challenges they face&mdash;from unpredictable rainfall to rising costs for labor and fertilizer.</p>
+      <p>Traceability matters to us because it is the only way to know that the price we pay actually reaches the people doing the work. For every lot we purchase, we can tell you the farm or washing station it came from, the variety of the plant, the altitude it was grown at, and how it was processed after harvest.</p>
+
+      <h3>Cupping and selection</h3>
+      <p>Once samples arrive at the roastery, the real tasting begins. Our team cups every sample blind, following a standardized protocol: the coffee is ground, steeped in hot water, and slurped from a spoon so that we can evaluate its fragrance, acidity, body, sweetness, and finish. A single harvest season can mean cupping more than four hundred samples, and only a small fraction make the cut. We look for coffees that score above 85 points on the Specialty Coffee Association scale, but more than any number, we look for coffees that taste distinctive&mdash;a Yirgacheffe that bursts with jasmine and bergamot, or a Huila that tastes like ripe red apple and brown sugar.</p>
+
+      <h3>Paying fairly, committing long-term</h3>
+      <p>We pay, on average, more than double the Fairtrade minimum price for our green coffee, and we publish those prices every year in our transparency report. Just as important as price is predictability. We sign multi-year purchasing commitments with most of our partners so they can plan ahead, invest in new drying beds or better fermentation tanks, and know there's a buyer waiting when the harvest comes in. Several of our partner farms have used that stability to improve quality season after season, which in turn earns them higher prices from us and from other buyers.</p>
+      <p>We also reinvest a portion of every bag sold into origin projects chosen by the communities themselves. In recent years that has funded a water filtration system for a cooperative in Sidama, agronomy training for young farmers in Nariño, and shade-tree planting to protect soil and biodiversity in the highlands of Huehuetenango.</p>
+
+      <h3>From green to roasted</h3>
+      <p>When the green coffee arrives in our warehouse, usually six to nine months after harvest, we store it in climate-controlled conditions to protect its freshness. Then our roasters develop a profile for each lot, running small test batches and cupping them side by side until we find the roast that best expresses the coffee's natural character. For most single origins, that means a lighter roast that preserves fruit and floral notes; for our espresso blends, we roast a little longer to build sweetness and body that stand up to milk.</p>
+      <p>We roast in small batches five days a week and ship within 48 hours of roasting, so the coffee you brew at home or order in our caf&eacute;s is as fresh as it can possibly be. It's a slower, more expensive way to run a coffee company&mdash;but every time we taste a new harvest from a farmer we've worked with for a decade, we're reminded exactly why we do it.</p>
+    </div>
